{{c|{{smaller|Flowering Branch of ''Vanilla planifolia.''<br>''a'', the fruit.}}}}
{{smaller|''V. planifolia'' is by far the largest source; but other species, as ''V. aromatica'' and ''V. grandiflora'', are also grown for use. Vanilla is most largely produced in Mexico, the product being obtained to a great extent from the wild plant; but the plant is also found, either wild or in cultivation, in various parts of Central and South America, and is more or less grown in many warm countries, notably in Mauritius and the Seychelles, Java, and Tahiti. On the isthmus of Pana-}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller|and Louisiana. It is a rather tall erect plant with numerous small rose-purple heads in a cymose panicle. The leaves have a persistent vanilla-like fragrance, and are considerably used to improve the odor of tobacco. The root-leaves are much larger than the others, and gain for the plant the name also of ''deer’s-tongue'' or ''hound’s-tongue.''}}

'''vanilla-bean''' (vạ̄-nil′ä̤-bēn), ''n.'' The fruit of the plant vanilla. See ''vanilla'', 1 and 2.

'''vanilla-grass''' (vạ̄-nil′ä̤-grȧs), ''n.'' A grass of the genus ''Hierochloë'', chiefly ''H. borealis;'' holy-grass. The large-leafed vanilla-grass is ''H. macrophylla'' of California. See ''Hierochloë''.

'''vanilla-plant''' (vạ̄-nil′ä̤-plant), ''n.'' '''1.''' See ''vanilla'', 1 and 3.—'''2.''' Same as ''wild vanilla'' (which see, under ''vanilla'').

'''vanillic''' (vạ̄-nil′ik), ''a.'' [< ''vanill(in)'' + ''-ic.''] Related to or derived from vanilla.—{{smaller|'''Vanillic acid''', a monobasic crystalline acid obtained by the oxidation of its aldehyde vanillin.}}

'''vanillin''' (vạ̄-nil′in), ''n.'' [< ''vanilla'' + ''-in''<sup>2</sup>.] The neutral odoriferous principle (C<sub>8</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O<sub>3</sub>) of vanilla. {{smaller|It forms crystalline needles having a hot, biting taste, soluble in hot water and in alcohol. It is now prepared artificially from coniferin and from oil of cloves, and used as a flavoring extract.}}

'''vanillism''' (vạ̄-nil′izm), ''n.'' [< ''vanilla'' + ''-ism.''] An affection observed among workers in vanilla, characterized by an itching papular eruption of the skin, irritation of the nasal mucous

readings.c2s3:
membrane, headache, vertigo, pains in the muscles, and great prostration. {{smaller|It is supposed to be due to a poisonous action of the vanilla or of the oil of cashew with which the pods are coated.}}

'''vanilloes''' (vā-nil′ōz), ''n.'' An inferior kind of vanilla obtained from ''Vanilla Pompona.''

'''vaniloquence†''' (vā-nil′ọ̄-kwens), ''n.'' [< L. ''vaniloquentia'', < *''vaniloquen(t-)s'', vaniloquent: see ''vaniloquent.''] Idle talk; vain babbling. ''Blount'', Glossographia (1670).

'''vaniloquent†''' (vā-nil′ọ̄-kwẹnt), ''a.'' [< L. *''vaniloquen(t-)s'', vaniloquent, < ''vanus'', empty, + ''loquen(t-)s'', ppr. of ''loqui'', speak, talk.] Talking idly or vainly. ''Bailey'', 1727.

'''vanish''' (van′ish), ''v. i.'' [< ME. ''vanisshen'', ''vanischen'', ''vaneschen'', ''vanschen'', < OF. ''vaniss-'' (stem of certain parts of *''vanir'' = It. ''vanire'', pres. ''vanisco''), < L. ''vanescere'', disappear, be in vain, < ''vanus'', empty, vain: see ''vain.''] '''1.''' To disappear quickly; pass from a visible to an invisible state; become imperceptible.
{{smaller block|The heavens shall ''vanish'' away like smoke. {{right|Isa. li. 6.}}
<poem>Of the ''vanished'' dream
No image was there left to him.</poem>
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, I. 96.}}}}
'''2.''' To pass out of view; pass beyond the limit of vision; disappear gradually; fade away.
{{smaller block|Now when she [the queen] could no longer detain the Empire from her son, not enduring to survive her glory, she ''vanisht'' out of sight. {{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 118.}}}}
'''3.''' To pass away; be annihilated or lost; be no more.
{{smaller block|Pick’d from the worm-holes of long-''vanish’d'' days.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., ii. 4. 86.}}}}

readings.c2s4:
{{smaller block|<poem>Before Atrides’ rage so sinks the foe,
Whole squadrons ''vanish'', and proud heads lie low.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Iliad, xi. 206.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
{{smaller block|All must feel that by his [Shelley’s] subtle sense of beauty he caught many a ''vanishing'' hue of earth and sky which no poet before him had noticed.
{{right|''J. C. Shairp'', Aspects of Poetry, p. 151.}}}}

'''4.''' To rise or be given off, as breath; exhale. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|A gentler judgment ''vanish’d'' from his lips.
{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., iii. 3. 10.}}}}

'''5.''' In ''math.'', to become zero.—{{smaller|'''Vanishing circle.''' See ''circle.''—'''Vanishing fraction''', in ''alg.'' See ''fraction.''—'''Vanishing line''', in ''persp.'', the line which represents the line at infinity in which any given plane cuts all parallel planes.—'''Vanishing plane''', in ''relief persp.'', the plane which represents the plane at infinity, and thus contains all vanishing points and vanishing lines.—'''Vanishing point''', in ''persp.'', the point which represents the point at infinity in which an imaginary line passing through the eye of the observer parallel to any straight line of an object to be drawn cuts that line produced and all parallel lines; hence, colloquially and in confusion with sense 5, the point or condition of disappearance of anything.}}
{{smaller block|The margin of profit has been reduced to ''vanishing-point.'' {{right|''Quarterly Rev.'', CXLV. 72.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Vanishing stress.''' See ''stress''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''vanish''' (van′ish), ''n.'' [< ''vanish'', ''v.''] In ''phonetics'', a sound with which another principal sound vanishes or ends, as the ''ē''-sound of ''ā'' (the

readings.c3s2:
i in ei as pronounced in ''veil''), or the ö-sound of ō (the ''u'' in ''ou'' as pronounced in ''soul'').

'''vanisher''' (van′ish-ėr), ''n.'' [< ''vanish'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who disappears or vanishes. ''Whittier.''

'''vanishingly''' (van′ish-ing-li), ''adv.'' In a vanishing manner; so as to vanish; imperceptibly: as, a certain probability is ''vanishingly'' small.

'''vanishment''' (van′ish-mẹnt), ''n.'' [< ''vanish'' + ''-ment.''] A vanishing.

'''Vanist''' (vā′nist), ''n.'' [< ''Vane'' (see def.) + ''-ist.''] One of the New England Antinomians, about 1637: so called from Sir Henry Vane, governor of Massachusetts Bay Colony in 1636.

'''vanitied''' (van′i-tid), ''a.'' [< ''vanity'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Affected with vanity. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|I am exasperated against your foolish, your low-''vanitied'' Lovelace.
{{right|''Richardson'', Clarissa Harlowe, IV. 86. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''vanity''' (van′ị-ti), ''n.''; pl. ''vanities'' (-tiz). [Early mod. E. ''vanitye'', ''vanitie''; < ME. ''vanitee'', ''vanite'', < OF. ''vanite'', ''vanitet'', F. ''vanité'' = Pr. ''vanitat'', ''vanetat'' = Sp. ''vanidad'' = Pg. ''vaidade'' = It. ''vanità'', < L. ''vanita(t-)s'', emptiness, vanity, < ''vanus'', empty, vain: see ''vain''.] '''1.''' The character or state of being vain. {{smaller|(''a'') Worthlessness; futility; falsity; unsubstantialness; unrealness; illusion; deception; emptiness; folly; want of substance to satisfy desire; hollowness.}}
{{smaller block|Nothing, God wot, but ''vanitee'' in sweven is.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Nun’s Priest’s Tale, l. 102.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Vanity'' of ''vanities'', saith the preacher, all is ''vanity''.
{{right|Eccles. i. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|All was ''vanity'', feeding the wind, and folly.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Urn-burial, v.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') The desire of indiscriminate admiration; inflation of}}

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller|mind upon slight ground; empty pride, inspired by an overweening conceit of one’s personal attainments or adornments, and making its possessor anxious for the notice and applause of others.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>To be fair,
And nothing virtuous, only fits the eye
Of gaudy youth and swelling ''vanity.''</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Faithful Shepherdess, i. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|They were faine to let him goe on till all men saw his ''vanity.'' {{right|''Bradford'', Plymouth Plantation, p. 171.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Vanity'' is the cordial drop which makes the bitter cup of life go down.
{{right|''J. Adams'', in Josiah Quincy’s Figures of the Past, p. 78.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Ostentation; ambitious display; pompous vaunting; pride; vainglory.}}
{{smaller block|They . . . through their owne ''vanitye'' . . . doe thereupon build and enlarge many forged historyes of theyr owne antiquitye. {{right|''Spenser'', State of Ireland.}}}}
{{smaller block|When the superior acts out of a principle of ''vanity'', the dependant will be sure to allow it him.
{{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 202.}}}}
'''2.''' That which is vain; anything empty, visionary, or unsubstantial. {{smaller|(''a'') Empty pleasure; idle show; unsubstantial enjoyment; petty object of pride.}}
{{smaller block|The pomps and ''vanity'' of this wicked world.
{{right|''Book of Common Prayer'', Catechism.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>They are gilded and adulterate ''vanities.''</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'' (''and another''?), Prophetess, v. 3.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Think not, when woman’s transient breath is fled,
That all her ''vanities'' at once are dead.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', R. of the L., i. 52.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Fruitless desire or endeavor; effort which produces no result.}}
{{smaller block|It is a ''vanity'' to waste our days in the blind pursuit of knowledge. {{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, ii. 8.}}}}
